I, too, just became a hearse user for the MS-Windows
version of NetHack, and was happily surprised to
donate _one_ bones file on my first attempt and get
_three_ in return. My donation may have been the one
with the unIDed uncursed spellbook of identify, for
a character who by unknown means angered her god and
as a result starved to death in Mines Town, of all
places. I was waiting until I could bless the
spellbook before reading it, of course. Sigh.

A spellbook of ID being one of the "possible single
things making ascension likely", I hated dying not
knowing I had it.

Note: as delivered, hearse does _not_ remove the
bones files it copies from your system, you need to
set up an icon (or a batch file) that executes
hearse with a flag described in the hearse docs to
make that happen.

It would be "nice" if hearse came with its own,
non-generic icon, perhaps an old fashioned horse
drawn hearse wagon(*), but I expect having that happen
needs a fairly sophisticated MS-Windows programming
ability to integrate the icon into the executable.

Probably that (having your hearse-ward bones file go
away on your (source) system) is the case you want,
so that all the bones files you are using are
unpredictable ones to you.

I wonder, if I reuse the bones file left behind, and
leave bones from it again, whether hearse "will
accept" / "wants to accept" that derivative bones
file?

